<!doctype html>
{% comment %}
  ============================================================================
  layout/suf.liquid — the layout for rebuilt pages.
  ============================================================================

  Selected per template, not globally. A JSON template opts in with a top-level
  key; a Liquid template with a tag:

      { "layout": "suf", "sections": { ... } }        <- JSON
      {% layout 'suf' %}                              <- Liquid

  templates/password.json already uses the JSON form for layout/password.liquid,
  which is the proof this works on a Vintage theme.

  IT LOADS suf.css AND suf.js, AND NO INHERITED CSS OR JAVASCRIPT AT ALL.
  Not loaded, deliberately: jQuery, dt-framework.css, dt-custom.css,
  dt-plugins.js (Swiper), dt-theme.js, handlebars, select2, ajax-cart,
  lazysizes, swiper-bundle.min.css, dT_main.js, dT_main_app.js -- roughly
  850 KB that theme.liquid serves on every page.

  Consequences, all intended:

  * NO CART DRAWER. The header cart icon falls back to its own href and goes to
    the cart page. New pages link straight to checkout, so the drawer is not
    wanted. The header supplies its own nav drawer, in sections/suf-nav.liquid.
  * NO LAZYSIZES. Use native loading="lazy", not class="lazyload".
  * NO COOKIE BANNER yet. See docs/migration.md — resolve before any suf page
    is assigned to a live page handle.
  * Legacy sections render only as well as dt-framework.css happens to style
    them, and this layout does not load it. Prefer suf-* sections here.
{% endcomment %}
<html lang="{{ request.locale.iso_code }}">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="{{ settings.primary_color }}">

    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.shopify.com" crossorigin>

    {%- if canonical_url != blank -%}
      <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if settings.favicon != blank -%}
      <link rel="shortcut icon" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 32 }}" type="image/png">
    {%- endif -%}

    {% render 'SEO-tags' %}
    {% render 'social-meta-tags' %}

    {% comment %}
      TWO of the five faces. @font-face is lazy, so the other three cost
      nothing until something renders at that weight -- but PRELOADING IS NOT
      LAZY. It fetches at high priority before the browser knows the file is
      needed, so only what paints on first load belongs here: 400 for body,
      800 for every heading.

      600 renders only after a menu opens, 700 is 8 uses below the fold, 900 is
      2 uses on 2 pages. Preloading those three cost ~41 KB of contended
      bandwidth per page and earned Chrome's "preloaded but not used" warning.
    {% endcomment %}
    {{ 'hanken-grotesk-400.woff2' | asset_url | preload_tag: as: 'font', type: 'font/woff2', crossorigin: 'anonymous' }}
    {{ 'hanken-grotesk-800.woff2' | asset_url | preload_tag: as: 'font', type: 'font/woff2', crossorigin: 'anonymous' }}


    {{ 'suf.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

    {% comment %}
      Theme-editor values -> custom properties, after suf.css so they override
      the fallbacks compiled into it.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% render 'suf-tokens' %}

    {% comment %}
      Enables the scroll-in accents. See
      frontend/styles/components/_suf-motion.scss.

      INLINE AND IN THE HEAD. The resting state of an accent is opacity 0, so
      the stylesheet applies it only on a page that can undo it -- a JS failure
      then costs the animation rather than the content. It cannot live in the
      deferred assets/suf.js, which would paint them visible, hide them, then
      animate them back.

      One line, and it stays one line.
    {% endcomment %}
    <script>
      document.documentElement.dataset.sufMotion = '';
    </script>

    {% comment %}
      Hanken Grotesk on this layout only. theme.liquid stays on Arial
      permanently -- a settled decision, reasoning in snippets/suf-fonts.liquid.

      set_weights: false because this layout supplies its own. The snippet's
      `h1, h2, ...` is (0,0,1) and the base layer's `:where(.suf-body) h1` is
      ALSO (0,0,1), since :where() adds nothing -- and this renders after
      suf.css, so the TIE went to the snippet and held every heading at 700
      while the stylesheet asked for 800.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% render 'suf-fonts', apply: true, set_weights: false, family: "'Hanken Grotesk', Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" %}

    {% # theme-check-enable ParserBlockingScript %}


    {% comment %}
      Required. Shopify injects analytics, app embeds and the theme editor
      bridge here. Removing it breaks the editor and app blocks.
    {% endcomment %}
    {{ content_for_header }}
  </head>

  <body class="suf-body template-{{ template | replace: '.', ' ' | truncatewords: 1, '' | handle }}">
    <a class="suf-skip-link" href="#suf-main">Skip to content</a>

    {% comment %}
      The back-to-top link's target. A real element rather than href="#",
      which scrolls the viewport but leaves keyboard focus at the bottom of
      the page -- the next Tab then resumes in the footer, which is the
      opposite of what the control promises. tabindex="-1" makes it focusable
      by the fragment jump without putting it in the tab order.
    {% endcomment %}
    <span id="suf-top" tabindex="-1"></span>

    {% comment %}
      The reveal threshold, as an element. assets/suf-totop.js watches it with
      an IntersectionObserver: while it is in view the page is inside the first
      800px and the button stays hidden. Its height is the threshold, so the
      distance is set once in CSS rather than duplicated in JS.
    {% endcomment %}
    <span class="suf-totop__sentinel" aria-hidden="true"></span>

    {% comment %}
      The header. layout/theme.liquid renders this same section, so both
      layouts read one settings key: one logo, one menu, no drift.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% section 'suf-nav' %}

    <main id="suf-main" class="suf-main">
      {{ content_for_layout }}
    </main>

    {% section 'suf-footer' %}

    {% comment %}
      Back to top. Always visible, as in the mockup -- the legacy snippet
      revealed it on scroll with jQuery, which this layout does not load. If it
      should be revealed rather than persistent, that is a few lines in suf.js,
      not a reason to keep the old snippet.

      Styled provisionally: it takes the CTA colour so it moves with the rest
      of the chrome when the header and footer are rebuilt.
    {% endcomment %}
    <a class="suf-totop" href="#suf-top">
      <span class="suf-totop__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&uarr;</span>
      <span class="suf-visually-hidden">Back to top</span>
    </a>


    {% comment %} Net-new SUF scripts. Native ES module; type=module implies defer. {% endcomment %}
    <script src="{{ 'suf.js' | asset_url }}" type="module"></script>
  </body>
</html>
